The {{nihongo|'''Honda clan'''|本多氏|Honda-shi}} is a Sanese clan that became the central political and military force during the unification period of [[Okaiken]] and the subsequent Honda Shogunate. Before [[Honda Shigetada]] succeeded in unifying the archipelago at the [[Battle of Tone]] in 1600, the Honda clan claimed direct descent from the Imperial family through [[Emperor Mizuno]]. The clan's most consequential figure, Honda Shigetada, was revealed in 1601 to be '''Prince Fumihito''', the eldest son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], who had been abducted during the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558. Shigetada ascended the throne as '''Emperor Shirakawa''' in 1610, making the Honda clan the only house in Sanese history to produce both an emperor and a shogun from the same generation.

The Honda clan's rise from an obscure provincial house of 10,000 ''koku'' to the ruling dynasty of a unified Okaiken is one of the most remarkable trajectories in Sanese history. Under the reformer [[Honda Shigetatsu]] (c. 1515–1581), the clan adopted the [[kandaka system|''kandaka'']] coin system and merit-based promotion, tripling its revenue and military effectiveness. Shigetatsu's adoption of a temple foundling named "Ichimatsu"—the disguised Prince Fumihito—set in motion a chain of events that would end 158 years of civil war. Shigetada's military genius, exemplified in the [[Battle of Tone]] (1600), where 160,000 men fought in a single day and casualties may have reached 100,000, established the Honda as the paramount power in the archipelago.

The Dual Sovereignty system established by the [[Edict of the Two Realms]] in 1611 divided authority between the Imperial Line (spiritual and ceremonial) and the Shogunal Line (military and administrative). This system governed Okaiken for over 250 years, but its fatal geographic flaw—the 500-kilometre separation between the Shogun's capital at [[Okami]] and the Retired Emperor's seat at [[Toyozawa Castle|Toyozawa]]—enabled the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625, in which [[Ii Naotaka]] assassinated Emperor Shirakawa and besieged Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]] at [[Ichijōdani]]. The tragedy culminated in the death of both Shigetomo and Naotaka on June 18, 1625, and was resolved by the [[Great Reconciliation]], which established the [[Pax Honda]]—260 years of peace that lasted until 1871.

Today, the clan's members continue to be influential in Sanese politics. [[Honda Shigetsuma]], the current head of the clan, has served as [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] since 2021. His grandfather, [[Honda Tadashige]], was Prime Minister from 1963 to 1966, overseeing the "Sanese Economic Miracle," and his great-uncle, [[Honda Shigehara]], served as Prime Minister from 1990 to 1993. The Honda clan's legacy extends beyond politics into mythology, religion, and culture: Shigetomo was deified as the '''God of Seals''' (''Fūin no Kami''), Naotaka as the '''God of Vindication''' (''Benmei no Kami''), and the annual [[Red String Festival]] at Ichijōdani commemorates the bond between the two men whose brotherhood and betrayal defined the end of the Sengoku era.

==History==

===Origins (1090–1515)===

The Honda clan claims descent from the ancient Imperial family, specifically through [[Emperor Mizuno]], though definitive records from the early period are scarce due to the destruction of the clan's archives during the [[Taiju War]]. Traditionally, the clan served as minor provincial lords in [[Kazusa Province]] (modern [[Miyagi Prefecture]]), acting as retainers to the powerful [[Soga clan]].

The clan was founded by {{nihongo|[[Honda Takakage]]|本多高景}} in 1090, during the late [[Asama period]]. Takakage, a minor retainer of the Soga, was granted a small fief in the foothills east of the [[Tone River]] in recognition of military service during the Taiju War. The fief was modest—barely 10,000 ''koku'' in assessed yield—but its position astride the northern trade routes gave the Honda a strategic advantage that would prove significant in later centuries. Takakage established his residence at a small fortified compound near the modern town of Honjō, and the clan would remain based in this region for the next four centuries.

For the next four centuries, the Honda remained an obscure provincial house—too small to threaten their neighbors, too remote to attract the attention of the capital, and too cautious to risk the gambles that defined the Gekokujō era. The early Honda leaders are poorly documented. The clan's archives were destroyed during the Taiju War, and the surviving genealogies—compiled centuries later by the Shogunate Historiographical Bureau—are unreliable for the pre-Sengoku period. What is known is that the Honda maintained a precarious independence through a series of strategic marriages and alliances, periodically submitting to larger houses such as the Soga, the [[Taketoyo clan|Taketoyo]], and the [[Kojima clan|Kojima]], while never expanding beyond their modest domain.

The best-established succession runs from Takakage through a series of minor lords whose names and deeds are largely lost to history. By the early 16th century, the clan was led by {{nihongo|[[Honda Shigenobu]]|本多重信}}, a cautious administrator who maintained the clan's survival through a succession of alliances with larger houses—the Taketoyo to the south, the Kojima to the north—but never expanded the Honda's territory beyond its modest 10,000 ''koku'' domain. Shigenobu is remembered in clan records as a man of prudence rather than ambition, and it would fall to his son, Shigetatsu, to transform the Honda from a minor provincial house into a dynasty that would rule Okaiken.

===Honda Shigetatsu and the Gekokujō Reforms (c. 1515–1581)===

{{Main|Honda Shigetatsu}}

The clan's transformation began under {{nihongo|[[Honda Shigetatsu]]|本多重辰}} (c. 1515–1581), Shigenobu's son and successor, who inherited the clan leadership around 1535. Shigetatsu embodied the {{nihongo|[[Gekokujō]]|下克上}} ethos of the Sengoku era—the philosophy that the lowly could overcome the mighty through talent, ambition, and ruthlessness. His reforms were comprehensive and far-reaching:

* '''Monetization of the economy:''' Shigetatsu abandoned the Jofun Code's rice-based taxation in favor of the [[kandaka system|''kandaka'']] coin system, monetizing the Honda's position on the northern trade routes. The kandaka system assessed land not by its rice yield but by its monetary value, allowing the clan to participate more effectively in the commercial economy that was transforming the Sengoku era. This reform tripled the clan's revenue within a generation.
* '''Merit-based promotion:''' Shigetatsu promoted soldiers based on competence rather than birth, breaking with the traditional pattern of hereditary retainership that dominated most Sanese clans. This attracted talented ronin and dispossessed warriors from across the archipelago, building a cadre of capable officers whose loyalty was to ability rather than bloodline.
* '''Strategic patience:''' Shigetatsu practiced a disciplined strategic patience that stood in sharp contrast to the destructive campaigns of his contemporaries. Rather than expanding through conquest, he consolidated the Honda's position through trade, diplomacy, and selective alliance, building the economic and military infrastructure that would later enable Shigetada's rapid rise.

Under Shigetatsu's leadership, the Honda clan's revenue tripled and its military effectiveness grew far beyond what its modest territory would suggest. The clan's retainers were well-equipped, well-trained, and well-paid, and the Honda's strategic position on the northern trade routes gave them access to information and resources that larger but less commercially connected clans lacked.

====The Adoption of Ichimatsu====

Shigetatsu's most consequential act was the adoption of a temple foundling known only as {{nihongo|"Ichimatsu"|一松}}, whom he discovered at the [[Shōhei Temple]] in [[Mino Province]] around 1560. The boy, believed to be approximately two years old when found, was raised as a warrior and given the name [[Honda Shigetada]]. Shigetatsu raised the boy as his own son, training him in the martial arts, strategy, and administration, and grooming him as his successor.

The boy was in reality '''Prince Fumihito''', the eldest son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], abducted during the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of October 12, 1558. The Yuzawa Incident was a violent raid on the imperial residence at Yuzawa Palace, in which Ogasawara agents seized the two-year-old prince and abandoned him at the Shōhei Temple. The abduction was part of a broader Ogasawara strategy to destabilize the imperial family and consolidate their own power during the political chaos of the mid-16th century. The Ogasawara clan, having no use for an infant, simply abandoned the child, believing him too young to remember his identity or pose a threat.

Shigetatsu raised the prince unaware of his imperial lineage—a supreme irony in which the Gekokujō ethos of merit over birth accidentally restored the imperial bloodline it had displaced. The boy grew up as a commoner's son, trained in the harsh discipline of a minor provincial clan, with no knowledge of his royal birth. This upbringing would shape Shigetada's character profoundly: he was a warrior first and a prince second, and his empathy for the common people of Okaiken—born of his own upbringing among them—would later distinguish his reign from those of emperors raised in the sheltered luxury of the imperial court.

====Submission to the Ogasawara====

Shigetatsu submitted the Honda clan to [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] around 1565, securing an unusually autonomous vassal status through the {{nihongo|"Mikata Accord"|三方協定|Mikata Kyōtei}}. The Mikata Accord was a remarkable document for its time: it guaranteed the Honda clan's internal autonomy, allowed Shigetada to retain his own military forces and retainers, and exempted the Honda from certain forms of tribute, in exchange for military service and political loyalty. This degree of autonomy was almost unheard of in the Sengoku era, where vassalage typically meant near-total subordination, and it reflected both Shigetatsu's diplomatic skill and Terumasa's need for capable allies.

Shigetatsu served Terumasa as a reliable vassal on the [[Harujima]] front, providing troops and intelligence for the Ogasawara's campaigns of expansion. He was present at several minor engagements but never achieved the fame that would later come to his adopted son. His health began to decline in the late 1570s, and by 1581 he was largely bedridden. He died from illness on 18 November 1581, whereupon Shigetada succeeded him as clan leader at the age of 25.

''Historical note:'' Some early records refer to Shigetada's adoptive father as "Honda Shigenobu" rather than Shigetatsu. Modern scholarship attributes this to a scribal confusion: Shigetada was adopted into the Honda clan while Shigenobu was still the nominal clan head, but it was Shigetatsu—Shigenobu's son and the actual operative leader—who personally discovered, adopted, and raised the boy. The ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' (1680) corrected the record definitively.

===Honda Shigetada: The Warrior Who Surpassed Death (1581–1610)===

{{Main|Honda Shigetada|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

Honda Shigetada (1556–1625), born Prince Fumihito, was the adopted son of Honda Shigetatsu and the figure who transformed the Honda clan from a minor provincial house into the ruling dynasty of Okaiken. His life spanned the full arc of the Sengoku period's final decades: from obscure temple foundling to fearsome general, from unification conqueror to enthroned emperor. His military campaigns unified the archipelago after 158 years of civil war, and his political innovations—particularly the Dual Sovereignty system—shaped the governance of Okaiken for over 250 years.

====Early Military Career under Ogasawara Terumasa (1581–1592)====

=====Battle of Mazama (1581)=====

Shigetada's first major military engagement came almost immediately upon his succession as clan leader. The [[Battle of Mazama]] (1581) was Ogasawara Terumasa's decisive campaign to break the power of the Mazama Confederacy, a coalition of northern lords who had resisted Ogasawara expansion for over a decade. Shigetada, then 25 years old, was given command of the vanguard—a remarkable appointment for a newly succeeded clan leader of such modest domain.

The battle was fought in the Mazama Valley, where the Confederacy had fortified a series of positions along the river. Shigetada's vanguard assault broke the Confederacy's center, and his personal leadership—fighting at the head of his troops—inspired a rout that collapsed the entire defensive line. Among those who distinguished themselves in the battle was a young cavalry commander named [[Ii Naomasa]], whose ferocious charge through the Confederate flank earned him the nickname "Red Devil" (''Aka-oni''). This was the first time Shigetada and Naomasa fought together, though they would not become personally acquainted for another eight years.

The Battle of Mazama established Shigetada as a military commander of the first rank and earned him Terumasa's particular favor. He was granted additional territory in the aftermath, expanding the Honda domain from 10,000 to approximately 30,000 ''koku''—still modest by the standards of the great clans, but a significant increase that reflected his growing reputation.

=====Fall of the Taketoyo and Years of Wandering (1582–1587)=====

The period following the Battle of Mazama was one of instability for the Honda clan. The [[Taketoyo clan]], the Honda's long-standing southern ally, was destroyed in 1582 by a coalition of rival houses, exposing the Honda's southern flank. Shigetada was forced to navigate a series of shifting alliances and minor engagements, fighting to preserve the Honda's autonomy in an increasingly volatile political landscape.

Between 1582 and 1587, Shigetada's movements are poorly documented. Clan records suggest he spent time in [[Mino Province]], where he maintained contacts with the Shōhei Temple community that had raised him, and may have traveled extensively as a ronin, gathering intelligence and building a network of contacts that would later prove invaluable. This period of relative obscurity ended in 1587, when Terumasa launched the First Invasion of Sanzu, and Shigetada was recalled to active service.

=====First Invasion of Sanzu (1587–1590)=====

The [[First Invasion of Sanzu]] (1587–1590) was Ogasawara Terumasa's ambitious campaign to conquer the southern island of Sanzu, then ruled by the Mitsui clan and their allies. The campaign was brutal and protracted, fought across difficult mountainous terrain against determined resistance. Shigetada served as one of Terumasa's principal commanders, leading troops in a series of engagements that gradually wore down the Mitsui defenses.

During the Sanzu campaign, Shigetada demonstrated the strategic patience he had learned from Shigetatsu, avoiding unnecessary engagements and concentrating on cutting the enemy's supply lines and isolating their fortresses. This approach, while effective, was slow, and Terumasa grew impatient with the pace of conquest. The tension between Shigetada's methodical approach and Terumasa's desire for rapid victory would become a recurring theme in their relationship.

The campaign also saw the first use of massed matchlock infantry in the Sanzu theater, a tactical innovation that would later prove decisive at the Battle of Tone. Shigetada studied the matchlock's capabilities closely, developing tactics that integrated firearms with traditional cavalry and infantry—a synthesis that would become a hallmark of Honda military doctrine.

=====Meeting Ii Naomasa (January 1589)=====

The single most consequential personal encounter of Shigetada's early career occurred in January 1589, when he met [[Ii Naomasa]] in a weapons shop in the town of [[Nagashino]]. Naomasa, then approximately 28 years old, was a ronin of uncertain origins who had built a reputation as one of the fiercest cavalry commanders in the archipelago. The two men recognized each other from the Battle of Mazama, where Naomasa had served in the vanguard alongside Shigetada.

The meeting was apparently chance—both men were shopping for weapons independently—but it quickly developed into a deep personal bond. Shigetada was struck by Naomasa's martial skill, his direct manner, and his fierce loyalty once given. Naomasa, for his part, saw in Shigetada a leader worth following: a man of strategic vision, personal courage, and—crucially—the willingness to reward talent regardless of birth. Within weeks, Naomasa had entered Honda service, bringing with him a cadre of cavalry veterans who would form the core of the famous "Red Devil" regiment.

This meeting would prove to be one of the most consequential in Sanese history. The Shigetada–Naomasa partnership would become the military engine of Honda unification, and their bond—described by contemporaries as closer than brotherhood—would set the template for the next generation's tragedy: the parallel relationship between Shigetada's son Shigetomo and Naomasa's son Naotaka, whose brotherhood would end in mutual destruction at Ichijōdani.

=====The Otomo Castle Hostages (1590–1592)=====

In 1590, as part of the political arrangements following the First Invasion of Sanzu, Terumasa demanded that his principal vassals provide hostages to guarantee their loyalty. Shigetada was required to send his eldest son, the eight-year-old [[Honda Shigetomo]] (born October 10, 1582), to [[Otomo Castle]], where he would be held under the custody of the Ii clan. Ironically, the Ii clan's young heir, [[Ii Naotaka]] (childhood name "Manchiyo," born c. 1583), was also held at Otomo Castle as a hostage of the Ogasawara.

The two boys—Shigetomo and Naotaka—were placed in the same quarters and quickly became inseparable. Their friendship, forged in the crucible of shared captivity, was the deepest emotional bond of either boy's life. They trained together, studied together, and confided in each other about their fears and hopes. This childhood bond would become the emotional core of the Sengoku period's final tragedy: 35 years later, at the Siege of Ichijōdani, it would be Naotaka who fired the shot that killed Shigetomo, and Naotaka who cradled the dying Shogun in the mud and received his forgiveness.

=====The Honto-ji Incident (1592)=====

The [[Honto-ji Incident]] of 1592 was the political earthquake that shattered the Ogasawara hegemony and set Shigetada on the path to national power. Ogasawara Terumasa, the "Demon King" who had dominated the archipelago for three decades, was assassinated by his own chief of staff, [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], during a council meeting at the Honto-ji temple. The assassination was apparently motivated by Takigawa's ambition to seize power for himself, and by his discovery of Shigetada's hidden identity as Prince Fumihito—a piece of information that Takigawa would later weaponize.

Terumasa's death triggered a succession crisis within the Ogasawara domain. His heir, [[Takigawa Kazutoyo]], was an infant, and a council of regents was established to govern in his name, dominated by [[Mashita Morihiro]]. Shigetada, as one of the most capable remaining commanders, found himself in a precarious position: too powerful to be ignored, but not powerful enough to dominate the council. He withdrew to his domains to consolidate his forces and await developments.

=====Conflict with Takigawa Kazumasu (1592–1600)=====

The years between 1592 and 1600 were defined by the escalating rivalry between Shigetada and Takigawa Kazumasu, the assassin who had murdered Terumasa. Takigawa, a peasant-born general who had risen through sheer military talent, was a formidable opponent: cunning, ruthless, and possessed of a deep understanding of the political landscape. He controlled the Ogasawara heir, Kazutoyo, and used the child's nominal authority to legitimize his own power.

Takigawa's most dangerous weapon, however, was his knowledge of Shigetada's true identity. He had discovered, through unknown means, that Shigetada was Prince Fumihito, the lost imperial heir. This information was a double-edged sword: revealing it could either destroy Shigetada's credibility as a warrior (by marking him as an imperial pawn) or elevate him beyond Takigawa's reach (by giving him a claim to the throne). Takigawa chose to withhold the information, using it as leverage and planning to reveal it at the moment of maximum advantage to himself.

During this period, Shigetada conducted a series of campaigns that expanded his territory and built the military machine that would eventually triumph at Tone. These included:

======The Mitsui Campaign (1596)======

The [[Mitsui Campaign]] of 1596 was Shigetada's most significant military operation of the 1590s. The Mitsui clan, rulers of southern Sanzu, had been weakened by the First Invasion but had never been fully subjugated. Shigetada, operating with nominal authority from the Ogasawara regency, launched a second invasion of Sanzu with the goal of finally destroying the Mitsui.

The campaign was notable for the first major deployment of Ii Naomasa's "Red Devil" cavalry regiment, which had been training and equipping since Naomasa's entry into Honda service in 1589. Naomasa's cavalry, mounted on fast Sanese horses and armed with lances and matchlocks, proved devastating against the Mitsui's traditional infantry formations. The Red Devils' charge at the Battle of Mitsuihara broke the enemy's center and triggered a rout that ended the Mitsui clan's resistance.

The Mitsui Campaign established the Red Devil cavalry as the most feared military unit in the archipelago and confirmed Naomasa's reputation as the preeminent cavalry commander of his generation. It also gave Shigetada control of southern Sanzu, significantly expanding his resource base and providing a platform for future operations.

======Governor-General of Northern Yagikoshu (1597)======

Following the Mitsui Campaign, Shigetada was appointed Governor-General of Northern Yagikoshu, a position that gave him administrative authority over a large swath of territory and further increased his political standing. In this role, he began to develop the administrative systems that would later form the basis of the Shogunate's governance: standardized taxation, road maintenance, and a system of granaries to ensure food security during famines.

======The Mercy Lesson (c. 1593–1594)======

One of the most significant events of this period was not a military engagement but a moral lesson that would echo through the remainder of Sanese history. Following the death of [[Minato Yoshishige]], a retainer who had served Shigetada loyally for years, Shigetada was confronted with the question of what to do with Yoshishige's killers—a group of bandits who had ambushed the retainer on the road.

Shigetada's initial instinct, consistent with the martial ethos of the Sengoku era, was to execute the bandits publicly as a warning. But after reflection, he chose a different path. He pardoned the bandits, on the condition that they enter Honda service and dedicate their lives to protecting the roads they had once preyed upon. The decision was controversial among his retainers, who saw it as weakness, but Shigetada explained his reasoning in words that would be preserved and passed down through generations:

{{quote|''"Not everything has to end by the blade."''}}

This lesson—the '''mercy lesson'''—would become one of the most consequential moral teachings in Sanese history. Shigetada passed it to his son Shigetomo, who would carry it to the gates of Ichijōdani 31 years later, offering forgiveness to the very man who had assassinated his father and was besieging his castle. The mercy lesson is widely regarded as the moral foundation of the Great Reconciliation and the Pax Honda that followed.

======Invasion of Ishijima (1599–1600)======

The [[Invasion of Ishijima]] (1599–1600) was Shigetada's final campaign before the Battle of Tone. The Ishijima island chain, ruled by the [[Chōsokabe clan]], had long been a haven for pirates and a base for raids against the mainland. Shigetada, acting on his own authority rather than the Ogasawara regency's, launched a naval expedition to subjugate the islands.

The campaign was notable for the [[Battle of Yamanote]] (1599), a naval engagement in which Shigetada's fleet defeated the Chōsokabe navy in a battle fought in rough seas off the Ishijima coast. The victory was achieved through a combination of superior seamanship, the use of fireships, and the timely arrival of a squadron commanded by [[Anjō Motonobu]], another of the "Four Honda Heavenly Pillars" (''Honda Shitennō''). The Chōsokabe fleet was destroyed, and the invasion force landed unopposed.

The subsequent [[Siege of Matsubara]] (1599–1600) was a protracted land engagement that saw Shigetada's forces reduce the Chōsokabe's main fortress over a period of several months. The siege was notable for the extensive use of siege mining—tunnels dug beneath the fortress walls to collapse them—and for the death of [[Sakakibara Yasumasa]], another of the Four Heavenly Pillars, who was killed leading an assault on the outer walls. The Chōsokabe surrendered in early 1600, and the Ishijima islands were incorporated into the Honda domain.

======Death of Takigawa Kazumasu (June 18, 1600)======

While Shigetada was campaigning in Ishijima, events on the mainland were moving toward their climax. Takigawa Kazumasu, who had been consolidating his power as regent for the infant Kazutoyo, grew increasingly paranoid about Shigetada's growing strength. He began mobilizing forces for a direct confrontation, planning to destroy the Honda clan before Shigetada could return from Ishijima.

The plan backfired. Shigetada's allies in the capital alerted him to Takigawa's preparations, and he rushed back to the mainland, arriving ahead of Takigawa's forces. In a rapid campaign, Shigetada besieged Takigawa at [[Katsuura Castle]], where the assassin-regent had retreated. On June 18, 1600, Takigawa Kazumasu was killed during the Siege of Katsuura—ironically, on the same date that Shigetomo would later die at Ichijōdani. With Takigawa's death, the last obstacle to Shigetada's supremacy was removed, and the stage was set for the final confrontation with the Ogasawara remnant.

=====The Battle of Tone (October 21, 1600)=====

{{Main|Battle of Tone}}

The [[Battle of Tone]], fought on October 21, 1600, was the decisive engagement of the Sengoku period and the battle that established the Honda clan as the ruling dynasty of Okaiken. It was one of the largest and bloodiest battles in Sanese history, with over 160,000 combatants and casualties that may have reached 100,000.

The battle was fought between the Honda coalition, led by Shigetada and including the Four Heavenly Pillars (Ii Naomasa, Anjō Motonobu, Sakakibara Yasumasa's successor, and others), and the Ogasawara remnant, led by [[Mashita Morihiro]], the regent for the infant Takigawa Kazutoyo. The Ogasawara forces included a coalition of clans that had remained loyal to the Ogasawara memory, including the Tsutsui, the Koyama, and several northern houses.

The battle was fought on the plain east of the Tone River, a broad, flat expanse that allowed for the deployment of large formations. Shigetada positioned his forces in a defensive arc, with the Red Devil cavalry on the left flank, infantry in the center, and a concealed force of matchlock infantry on the right. The Ogasawara forces attacked at dawn, driving into the Honda center with heavy infantry.

The battle turned on two critical moments:

First, the '''defection of Koyama Hidemoto'''. Koyama, who commanded the Ogasawara right flank, had been in secret communication with Shigetada for weeks. At the critical moment, when the Ogasawara attack had reached its furthest penetration, Koyama ordered his forces to withdraw from the field and then turn against their former allies. This betrayal shattered the Ogasawara right flank and triggered a cascading collapse of their entire line.

Second, the '''Red Devil charge'''. Ii Naomasa, who had been waiting in reserve on the Honda left, launched his cavalry at the exposed Ogasawara center. The Red Devils, mounted on fast horses and armed with lances, struck the disorganized enemy infantry with devastating force. The charge broke the Ogasawara center and triggered a general rout.

The aftermath of the battle was catastrophic. The '''"Crimson Fog"'''—a phenomenon in which the blood and dust of the battlefield rose into the air and created a reddish mist that obscured the field—was reported by multiple sources. The fog was so thick that survivors described being unable to see more than a few feet in front of them, and many soldiers were killed by friendly fire in the confusion. The battle's casualties, estimated at approximately 100,000, made it one of the deadliest single-day engagements in human history.

The Battle of Tone established Shigetada as the undisputed master of Okaiken. Mashita Morihiro was captured and executed, and the Ogasawara remnant was dissolved. The infant Takigawa Kazutoyo was taken into Honda custody and raised as a ward of the clan. The battle also marked the effective end of the Sengoku period's military phase, though the political settlement would take another decade to formalize.

=====Restoration as Prince Fumihito (January 12, 1601)=====

The revelation of Shigetada's true identity came on January 12, 1601, when [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]—bedridden for nearly a decade and largely powerless during the Sengoku chaos—publicly recognized Shigetada as his lost son, Prince Fumihito. The recognition was the result of months of quiet negotiation between Shigetada's advisors and the imperial court, which had been aware of Shigetada's identity for some time but had been waiting for the political moment to acknowledge it.

The recognition was a political masterstroke. It gave Shigetada's rule a legitimacy that transcended mere military conquest: he was not simply a warlord who had seized power, but the rightful heir to the imperial throne, restored to his birthright after 43 years of exile. The recognition also solved a political problem for the imperial court, which had been desperate for a strong protector during the chaos of the Sengoku era.

The reaction among the Sanese lords was mixed. Some saw the recognition as a divine validation of Shigetada's conquest; others viewed it as a political manipulation. The Ii clan, in particular, had a complex reaction: Ii Naomasa, who had served Shigetada for over a decade, was overjoyed that his lord's true identity had been recognized, but the revelation also complicated the Ii clan's own political position, as their relationship with the Honda shifted from vassalage to service to an imperial prince.

=====Pacification Campaigns (1602–1605)=====

Following the Battle of Tone and his recognition as Prince Fumihito, Shigetada launched a series of pacification campaigns to consolidate his control over the archipelago. These campaigns were smaller and less bloody than the Tone campaign, focusing on reducing isolated fortresses and bringing recalcitrant lords into submission.

======Battle of Nakasendo (March 1602)======

The [[Battle of Nakasendo]] (March 1602) was fought between Shigetada's forces, commanded by his son Honda Shigetomo, and a remnant of Ii clan forces that had refused to accept the new order. The battle was Shigetomo's first independent command, and his performance—aggressive, relentless, and merciless—earned him the nickname "the Punisher" (''Batsujin''). The Ii forces were defeated and scattered, but the battle sowed the seeds of future conflict by hardening the Ii clan's resentment against the Honda.

======Battle of Ishikawada (March 1602)======

The [[Battle of Ishikawada]] (March 1602) followed almost immediately after Nakasendo. Shigetomo, fresh from his victory, was ordered to intervene in a dispute between Ii clan factions. His intervention—this time on the side of the Ii clan's moderate faction, led by [[Ii Naoyoshi]]—rebuilt the bridge between the Honda and the Ii, at least temporarily. The battle demonstrated Shigetomo's capacity for political as well as military action, though his "Punisher" reputation from Nakasendo continued to shadow him.

======Ishijima Highland Campaign (1602–1604)======

The [[Ishijima Highland Campaign]] (1602–1604) was a protracted counterinsurgency operation in the highlands of Ishijima, where remnants of the Chōsokabe and their allies had retreated after the 1599–1600 invasion. The campaign was brutal, fought in difficult mountain terrain against guerrilla fighters who used their knowledge of the landscape to devastating effect.

The campaign's most significant engagement was the [[Battle of Aonashiyama]] (1604), in which Ii Naomasa led an assault on a fortified highland position. Naomasa, fighting at the head of his Red Devil cavalry as always, was wounded by a matchlock ball. The wound was not immediately fatal, but in the unsanitary conditions of a mountain campaign, it became infected. Naomasa continued to fight for several weeks, refusing to leave the field, but the infection worsened.

Ii Naomasa died on May 21, 1604, from the infected gunshot wound. His final words, preserved in the ''Kazumaiki'' and the ''Mitsukoji'', were a warning to Shigetada about the dangers of misplaced generosity:

{{quote|''"Do not be too generous with the wolves, my lord. They will eat the hand that feeds them, and come back for the arm."''}}

The warning was widely interpreted as referring to the Ii clan itself—Naomasa's own son, Naotaka, whom Naomasa feared was growing dangerously ambitious—and as a prophecy of the Kinzō Incident, which would occur 21 years later. Naomasa's death was a devastating blow to Shigetada, who had relied on his "Red Devil" for 15 years and considered him the closest thing to a brother he had ever known. The death also removed the one figure who might have restrained Naotaka's ambitions, and its absence would prove catastrophic.

=====Relocation to Okami (1605–1610)=====

In the years between Naomasa's death and his ascension as emperor, Shigetada consolidated his political position. He relocated his capital from the Honda clan's ancestral lands in Kazusa Province to the city of [[Okami]], a more central location that was better positioned to govern the entire archipelago. Okami would become the political and administrative center of Okaiken for the next 260 years, and its development into a major city was one of Shigetada's most enduring legacies.

During this period, Shigetada also began to develop the administrative systems that would later form the basis of the Shogunate. He established a system of provincial governors, standardized weights and measures, and began the construction of a road network that would connect the major cities of the archipelago. These administrative innovations, while less dramatic than his military campaigns, were equally important in establishing the long-term stability of Honda rule.

=====Ascension as Emperor Shirakawa (November 8, 1610)=====

On November 8, 1610, Shigetada ascended the throne as '''Emperor Shirakawa''', becoming the 108th Emperor of Mitoyagi. The ascension was a moment of extraordinary historical significance: a temple foundling, raised as a commoner's son in a minor provincial clan, had risen to become the rightful emperor of Okaiken. The ceremony, held at the Imperial Palace in [[Mitoyagi]], was attended by lords from across the archipelago and was marked by an unusual degree of popular celebration, as the common people—who had suffered through 158 years of civil war—saw in Shigetada's ascension the promise of peace.

The ascension also formalized the Honda clan's unique position in Sanese history. The clan that had served as minor provincial lords for four centuries had produced an emperor, and would soon produce a shogun as well. No other clan in Sanese history could claim both distinctions, and the Honda clan's dual connection to both the imperial and military establishments gave it a legitimacy that no other house could match.

====The Dual Sovereignty System (1611)====

{{Main|Edict of the Two Realms}}

Emperor Shirakawa's most significant political innovation was the [[Edict of the Two Realms]] (1611), which established the system of '''Dual Sovereignty''' that would govern Okaiken for over 250 years. The system divided authority between two branches of the Honda family:

* '''The Imperial Line:''' Descended from Emperor Shirakawa's eldest son, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Prince Takahito]] (Emperor Daigo), who held spiritual and ceremonial authority. The Imperial Line was based at the Imperial Palace in [[Mitoyagi]] and retained the divine authority of the throne.
* '''The Shogunal Line (Okami Branch):''' Descended from Emperor Shirakawa's second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]], who held military and administrative authority as the [[Honda Shoguns|Shogun]]. The Shogunal Line was based at [[Okami Castle]] and controlled the apparatus of government—the military, the taxation system, and the provincial administration.

The Dual Sovereignty system was a political compromise designed to resolve the tension between the imperial legitimacy that Shigetada's ascension provided and the practical need for military governance. By separating the spiritual and military functions of government, the system allowed the emperor to retain his divine authority while delegating the day-to-day governance to the Shogun. This was a novel solution to a problem that had plagued Sanese politics for centuries: the tension between the symbolic authority of the throne and the practical power of the military.

However, the system contained a '''fatal geographic flaw'''. Emperor Shirakawa, upon retiring from active governance, established his residence at [[Toyozawa Castle]], approximately 500 kilometres from the Shogun's capital at Okami. This distance meant that communication between the two sovereigns took days, and in times of crisis, the delay could be catastrophic. The 500-kilometre gap between Okami and Toyozawa would, in 1625, enable the Kinzō Incident: by the time the Shogun learned of the assault on Toyozawa, the Emperor was already dead.

====Repression of Christianity====

During Emperor Shirakawa's reign, the repression of Christianity was a significant policy initiative. Christian missionaries, who had been active in Okaiken since the late 16th century, were viewed with suspicion by the new regime as potential agents of foreign influence. The repression began with restrictions on missionary activity in 1612 and escalated to outright prohibition by 1614. Christian converts were required to renounce their faith, and those who refused faced imprisonment or execution.

The repression of Christianity was part of a broader policy of cultural consolidation that sought to unify the archipelago under a single set of cultural and religious norms. The policy was controversial, even within the Honda clan, and contributed to the tensions that would eventually erupt in the Kinzō Incident.

====Retirement (1619)====

In 1619, Emperor Shirakawa formally retired from active governance, ceding day-to-day authority to Shogun Honda Shigetomo. He retired to Toyozawa Castle, where he continued to exercise spiritual authority and to advise the Shogun on matters of policy. His retirement, however, created the geographic separation that would prove fatal: with the Emperor at Toyozawa and the Shogun at Okami, the two centers of authority were 500 kilometres apart, and the communication delay between them would enable the Kinzō Incident.

During his retirement, Emperor Shirakawa focused on spiritual and cultural pursuits. He patronized the arts, supported the construction of temples, and continued to develop the ideological framework of Dual Sovereignty. He also maintained a close correspondence with Shigetomo, offering advice and guidance on matters of governance—though his letters, preserved in the ''Honda Letters'' (declassified 2016), reveal a growing concern about the pace of reform and the resistance it was generating.

====The Kinzō Reforms (1623–1625)====

{{Main|Kinzō Reforms}}

The '''Kinzō Reforms''' (1623–1625) were a series of ambitious administrative reforms initiated by Shogun Honda Shigetomo and supported by Emperor Shirakawa. The reforms were named after the Kinzō—the gold treasury of the Shogunate—and were designed to address the fiscal and administrative challenges of the new regime. The three principal reforms were:

# '''The National Land Survey''' (1623): A comprehensive federal land assessment that sought to catalog and evaluate every parcel of land in the archipelago. The survey was intended to provide the Shogunate with accurate information about land ownership, agricultural productivity, and tax liability, and to eliminate the corruption and inefficiency that had characterized the previous system of land management. The survey was deeply unpopular among the samurai class, as it threatened to expose the many cases in which samurai lords had underreported their land holdings to avoid taxation.
# '''The Rice Exchange Decree''' (1624): A reform of the taxation system that replaced the rice-based ''koku'' assessment with a currency-based system. The decree established the [[Okami Rice Exchange]], a centralized market for the trading of rice and other commodities, and required all taxes to be paid in currency rather than in kind. The decree was designed to monetize the economy and increase the Shogunate's fiscal flexibility, but it was deeply controversial, as it disrupted the traditional relationship between samurai and their rice-producing domains.
# '''The Abolition of Swords''' (proposed 1625): A proposal to restrict the carrying of swords to the samurai class, denying weapons to commoners. The proposal was part of a broader policy of social stratification that sought to formalize the division between samurai, peasant, artisan, and merchant. The proposal was never fully implemented during Shigetomo's lifetime, but it generated intense opposition from the Ii clan and other warrior houses, who saw it as a threat to their traditional privileges.

The Kinzō Reforms were the immediate trigger of the Kinzō Incident. The Ii clan, which had been growing increasingly restive under Honda rule, saw the reforms as a direct threat to their land rights and traditional privileges. Ii Naotaka, who had succeeded his father Naomasa as clan head in 1604, began to mobilize his forces in response to the reforms, setting the stage for the final catastrophe of the Sengoku era.

====The Kinzō Incident (June 6, 1625)====

{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

The [[Kinzō Incident]] (June 6, 1625) was the climactic catastrophe of the Sengoku period—the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa by Ii Naotaka's forces and the triggering of the final military crisis of the era. The incident was the culmination of years of mounting tension between the Honda Shogunate and the Ii clan, and its consequences would reshape the political landscape of Okaiken.

On the morning of June 6, 1625, Ii Naotaka's forces launched a surprise assault on [[Toyozawa Castle]], where the 69-year-old Emperor Shirakawa was residing in retirement. The assault was overwhelming: Naotaka had assembled a force of several thousand troops, and the Toyozawa garrison, numbering only a few hundred, was quickly overwhelmed.

Emperor Shirakawa, despite his advanced age, armed himself and took position at the '''Bridge of Heaven'''—a stone bridge that connected the inner and outer wards of the castle. There, in a last stand that would become one of the most celebrated moments in Sanese history, the Emperor fought against overwhelming numbers. He was shot by seven bullets—matchlock balls that struck his body as he stood defending the bridge. He died at the Bridge of Heaven, the emperor who had unified the archipelago struck down by the son of his closest companion.

The news of the assassination reached Shogun Shigetomo at Okami several days later, delayed by the 500-kilometre distance that was the fatal flaw of the Dual Sovereignty system. Shigetomo, already under intense psychological pressure from the political crisis, was devastated. The Honda Letters (declassified 2016) reveal the depth of his psychological deterioration in the days following the assassination—a deterioration that would culminate in the Siege of Ichijōdani and his own death 12 days later.

===Honda Shigetomo: The Punisher Who Learned Mercy (1610–1625)===

{{Main|Honda Shigetomo}}

Honda Shigetomo (October 10, 1582 – June 18, 1625) was the second Shogun of Okaiken and one of the most complex and tragic figures in Sanese history. His life arc—from the "Punisher" of his early military career to the reformist administrator of his Shogunate, and finally to the tragic martyr of Ichijōdani—encapsulates the moral transformation of the Sengoku era from an age of war to an age of governance. His death, offering forgiveness to the man who had assassinated his father and was besieging his castle, is widely regarded as the moral climax of Sanese history.

====Childhood Captivity at Otomo Castle (1590–1592)====

Shigetomo's formative years were defined by his captivity at [[Otomo Castle]] (1590–1592), where he was held as a hostage guaranteeing his father Shigetada's loyalty to the Ogasawara regime. The hostage system was a standard feature of Sengoku-era politics, but for the eight-year-old Shigetomo, the experience was traumatic: he was separated from his family, placed under the custody of strangers, and acutely aware that his life depended on his father's political behavior.

At Otomo Castle, Shigetomo was placed in the same quarters as [[Ii Naotaka]] (childhood name "Manchiyo"), the young heir of the Ii clan, who was also held as a hostage. The two boys, close in age (Shigetomo born 1582, Naotaka born c. 1583), quickly became inseparable. They trained together in the martial arts, studied together under the castle's tutors, and confided in each other about their fears, their loneliness, and their hopes for the future.

The friendship between Shigetomo and Naotaka was the deepest emotional bond of either boy's life. It was also the emotional foundation of the Sengoku period's final tragedy: 35 years later, at the Siege of Ichijōdani, it would be Naotaka who fired the shot that killed Shigetomo, and Naotaka who cradled the dying Shogun in the mud, receiving his forgiveness and his bloodstained letter. The bond forged at Otomo Castle—between two boys who were both prisoners and both princes—would endure through war, political rivalry, and betrayal, and would find its final expression in the mud of Ichijōdani.

====Military Career: The "Punisher" (1600–1610)====

Shigetomo's early military career was marked by a brutality that earned him the nickname "the Punisher" (''Batsujin''). His first independent command, at the [[Battle of Nakasendo]] (March 1602), was a victory over Ii clan forces that was characterized by relentless pursuit and merciless treatment of defeated enemies. Shigetomo's forces gave no quarter, and the battle's casualties were disproportionately high.

The "Punisher" persona was a product of several factors: Shigetomo's need to prove himself as a warrior in his own right (apart from his father's shadow), the martial ethos of the Sengoku era, and perhaps a psychological compensation for the vulnerability he had felt as a hostage. Whatever the cause, the Punisher reputation would shadow Shigetomo for the rest of his life, and his later transformation into a reformist administrator—and ultimately into a figure of mercy—would be all the more remarkable for the contrast.

The [[Battle of Ishikawada]] (March 1602), which followed almost immediately after Nakasendo, showed a different side of Shigetomo. Ordered to intervene in a dispute between Ii clan factions, Shigetomo chose to support the moderate faction led by [[Ii Naoyoshi]], rather than destroying the Ii entirely. This decision—motivated in part by his childhood friendship with Naotaka—rebuilt the bridge between the Honda and the Ii, at least temporarily. The battle demonstrated Shigetomo's capacity for political as well as military action, and hinted at the more nuanced approach that would characterize his later career.

During the [[Ishijima Highland Campaign]] (1602–1604), Shigetomo served alongside Ii Naomasa, the Red Devil, in the protracted counterinsurgency operation in the Ishijima highlands. The campaign was brutal and frustrating, fought in difficult terrain against elusive guerrilla fighters. Shigetomo's performance was solid but not spectacular, and the campaign's most significant event was the death of Naomasa from an infected gunshot wound on May 21, 1604—a loss that affected both Shigetomo and his father deeply.

====Shogun: The Reformist Administrator (1610–1625)====

When Shigetada ascended as Emperor Shirakawa on November 8, 1610, Shigetomo was appointed Shogun, assuming the military and administrative authority of the Dual Sovereignty system. His appointment marked the beginning of a transformation from the "Punisher" of his youth to the reformist administrator of his maturity—a transformation that would culminate in the mercy of Ichijōdani.

=====Administrative Innovations=====

As Shogun, Shigetomo implemented a series of administrative innovations that laid the foundation for 260 years of Honda rule:

* '''The Okami Rice Exchange''' (1624): A centralized commodity market that standardized rice prices and provided a mechanism for converting rice taxes to currency. The Exchange was the economic engine of the Shogunate, providing the fiscal flexibility needed to fund the Kinzō Reforms and the broader apparatus of government.
* '''The Hokuto Omi–Shinshu Road''': A major highway connecting the northern provinces to the capital at Okami, facilitating trade, communication, and the movement of troops. The road was one of the most ambitious infrastructure projects of the era and remained a key transportation artery for centuries.
* '''Provincial governance system''': A standardized system of provincial governors, appointed by the Shogun and subject to regular review, that replaced the ad hoc arrangements of the Sengoku era with a rational, centralized administration.
* '''Codification of law''': The systematic codification of criminal and civil law, replacing the patchwork of local customs and feudal privileges that had governed the archipelago during the Sengoku era with a unified legal code.

=====The Lacuña Meeting (1615)=====

The '''Lacuña Meeting''' of 1615 was a pivotal moment in Shigetomo's personal transformation. According to the ''Mitsukoji'' and confirmed by the ''Honda Letters'', Shigetomo met with a former enemy—a retainer named [[Lacuña]] who had fought against the Honda at the Battle of Tone and had been captured and imprisoned—during an inspection tour of the provinces.

The meeting was apparently unplanned. Shigetomo, visiting the prison where Lacuña was held, was confronted by the prisoner, who asked to be executed rather than continue in captivity. Shigetomo, moved by the man's dignity and despair, chose instead to pardon him and offer him a position in the provincial administration. The decision was controversial—many of Shigetomo's retainers saw it as weakness, a reversion to the naive mercy that had no place in the harsh world of Sanese politics—but Shigetomo defended it by invoking his father's mercy lesson: "Not everything has to end by the blade."

The Lacuña Meeting is widely regarded as the symbolic turning point in Shigetomo's transformation from the "Punisher" to the reformist Shogun. It demonstrated that the mercy lesson, taught by Shigetada after the Minato Yoshishige incident, had taken root in his son's heart, and it foreshadowed the greater act of mercy that Shigetomo would offer at Ichijōdani ten years later.

====The Kinzō Incident and Psychological Deterioration (June 1625)====

{{Main|Kinzō Incident|Honda Letters}}

The assassination of Emperor Shirakawa on June 6, 1625, triggered a catastrophic psychological deterioration in Shogun Shigetomo. The ''Honda Letters''—Shigetomo's personal correspondence between May 18 and June 17, 1625, declassified on April 8, 2016—reveal the full extent of this deterioration, which the official ''Mitsukoji'' chronicle had deliberately obscured.

The Honda Letters document three distinct stages of Shigetomo's psychological state:

'''Stage 1 (May 18 – June 5): Bureaucratic Frenzy.''' In the weeks before the Kinzō Incident, Shigetomo's letters reveal a state of intense bureaucratic activity—long letters about the details of the Kinzō Reforms, the National Land Survey, and the Rice Exchange Decree. The letters are detailed, precise, and almost manic in their energy. Shigetomo was working at an extraordinary pace, driving himself and his subordinates to complete the reforms before the political opposition could coalesce. The letters from this period show no sign of the breakdown to come—only an intense, focused determination that, in retrospect, appears to have been a form of psychological overcompensation.

'''Stage 2 (June 6 – 15): Psychotic Breakdown.''' The news of his father's assassination on or about June 9 (delayed by the 500-kilometre distance from Toyozawa to Okami) triggered a catastrophic psychological collapse. Shigetomo's letters from this period are fragmented, incoherent, and marked by extreme emotional volatility. He alternates between rage, grief, and desperate rationalization, sometimes within the same letter. He writes about his father in the present tense, as if unable to accept his death. He writes about Naotaka with a mixture of fury and anguish, cursing the man who was once his closest friend. He writes about the mercy lesson—his father's teaching that "not everything has to end by the blade"—and struggles to reconcile it with the reality of his father's violent death.

The letters from this period also reveal Shigetomo's growing paranoia. He writes about threats to his own life, about betrayal within his own government, about the possibility that the Kinzō Reforms were a mistake that had provoked the very violence they were designed to prevent. He writes about the '''tetsu-kage'''—the "iron shadow"—his term for the hard institutional crust of the Shogunate that concealed the soft silt of human frailty beneath. The tetsu-kage concept, which Shigetomo developed during this period of breakdown, would later become an important concept in Sanese political philosophy, describing the gap between institutional design and human reality.

'''Stage 3 (June 16 – 17): The Final 48 Hours.''' In the last 48 hours of Shigetomo's life, the letters undergo a remarkable transformation. The fragmentation and paranoia of Stage 2 give way to a sudden, eerie clarity. Shigetomo's final letters are calm, measured, and deeply reflective. He writes about his childhood at Otomo Castle, about his friendship with Naotaka, about the days they spent training and studying and dreaming together. He writes about the mercy lesson, and about his determination to carry it to its ultimate conclusion. He writes the '''bloodstained letter'''—the formal decree offering Naotaka the Higo domain (1 million ''koku'' in modern [[Kagami Prefecture]], in the deep south of the archipelago) and, more importantly, his personal forgiveness.

The bloodstained letter exists in two versions. The '''first version''', found in Shigetomo's archives and never sent, is aggressive and confrontational, demanding Naotaka's surrender and threatening total destruction. The '''second version''', the one Shigetomo actually carried with him to Ichijōdani, is a formal decree offering the Higo domain and a personal letter of forgiveness. The contrast between the two versions reveals the depth of Shigetomo's internal struggle: the "Punisher" of his youth wrote the first version, the merciful Shogun of his maturity wrote the second.

====The Siege of Ichijōdani (June 12–18, 1625)====

{{Main|Siege of Ichijoudani}}

The [[Siege of Ichijōdani]] (June 12–18, 1625) was the final military engagement of the Sengoku period and the event that produced the moral climax of Sanese history. The siege was fought between Shogun Shigetomo's forces and the army of Ii Naotaka, who had seized Toyozawa Castle and assassinated Emperor Shirakawa on June 6.

=====The March to Ichijōdani=====

Shigetomo marched from Okami on approximately June 10, leading a force of several thousand troops toward Ichijōdani, where Naotaka had established his headquarters. The march was rapid—Shigetomo drove his forces hard, covering the distance in two days—but it was also marked by a strange calm. According to the ''Honda Letters'', Shigetomo spent the march in silence, carrying the bloodstained letter in a leather pouch close to his body. His retainers, who had witnessed his psychological deterioration, were uncertain whether to be reassured or alarmed by his composure.

=====Battle of the Ichijōdani Rapids (June 12)=====

The first engagement of the siege was the [[Battle of Ichijōdani Rapids]] (June 12), fought at a river crossing near the town. Shigetomo's vanguard, attempting to cross the rapids, was met by Ii clan forces who held the opposite bank. The battle was fierce and costly, with both sides taking heavy casualties, but Shigetomo's forces eventually forced the crossing and established a bridgehead on the Ii side of the river.

=====Battle of Nishi-ku (June 14–15)=====

The [[Battle of Nishi-ku]] (June 14–15) was fought in the western district of Ichijōdani, where Naotaka had fortified a series of positions. The battle was a grinding, house-to-house engagement that cost both sides heavily. Shigetomo's forces gradually pushed the Ii defenders back toward the castle, but the resistance was fierce and the casualties were high.

=====The Castle Siege (June 16–18)=====

By June 16, Shigetomo's forces had surrounded Naotaka's castle at Ichijōdani. The siege was now in its final phase: the castle's defenses were strong, but the Ii forces were outnumbered and running low on supplies. Shigetomo, rather than ordering an immediate assault, chose to negotiate, sending messengers to Naotaka with the offer of the Higo domain and his personal forgiveness.

Naotaka's response is not recorded in the official ''Mitsukoji'', but the ''Honda Letters'' suggest that he rejected the initial offer and prepared for a final assault. The reasons for his rejection are unclear—pride, fear, distrust of Shigetomo's sincerity, or a combination of all three—but the rejection set the stage for the final catastrophe.

=====The Shot (June 18)=====

On the morning of June 18, 1625, Shigetomo ordered a final assault on the castle. He led the assault personally, fighting at the head of his troops as his father had done at the Bridge of Heaven 12 days earlier. As he reached the castle gate, a 15mm matchlock ball struck him in the chest.

The shot was fired from the castle walls. Whether Naotaka himself fired it, or whether it was fired by one of his soldiers, remains a matter of historical debate. The ''Mitsukoji'' states that the shot was fired by "an unknown soldier," but the ''Honda Letters'' and several later accounts suggest that Naotaka himself may have been the shooter. The question is ultimately unanswerable, but its emotional resonance is profound: the man who had been Shigetomo's closest childhood friend may have been the one who killed him.

=====The Letter and "Seal It" (Fūjiro)=====

As Shigetomo lay dying in the mud at the castle gate, Naotaka—whether from guilt, grief, or some combination of both—came down from the castle walls and cradled the dying Shogun in his arms. The scene that followed is one of the most emotionally charged in all of Sanese history, preserved in multiple sources including the ''Mitsukoji'', the ''Honda Letters'', and the ''Kazumaiki''.

Shigetomo, bleeding from the matchlock wound, reached into the leather pouch at his side and drew out the bloodstained letter. He pressed it into Naotaka's hands. The letter, already prepared before the assault, offered Naotaka the Higo domain—1 million ''koku'' in modern Kagami Prefecture, in the deep south of the archipelago—and, more importantly, Shigetomo's personal forgiveness for the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa.

The letter was soaked in blood and mud. Shigetomo's blood, from the matchlock wound, had seeped through the leather pouch and saturated the paper. The physical artifact—preserved to this day as the most sacred treasure of the Ii clan—is a blood-soaked, mud-stained document that took researchers two years to fully decipher after the 2016 declassification of the Honda Letters.

Shigetomo's final word to Naotaka was:

{{quote|''"Seal it."'' (封じろ, ''Fūjiro'')}}

The meaning of "Seal it" has been debated for centuries. The most widely accepted interpretation is that Shigetomo was instructing Naotaka to "seal" the cycle of vengeance—to end the bloodshed, to accept the forgiveness, and to ensure that the violence that had claimed Emperor Shirakawa's life would not claim any more. The word ''fūjiro'' is a command form of ''fūjin'' (封印), meaning "to seal" or "to put an end to," and its use as a dying word has become one of the most iconic moments in Sanese cultural memory.

"Seal it" is also interpreted as a reference to Shigetomo's later deification as the '''God of Seals''' (''Fūin no Kami''). The god of seals is the god of endings, of closures, of the final word that puts an end to a cycle. By instructing Naotaka to "seal it," Shigetomo was, in effect, defining his own divine role: the one who seals the cycle of violence and ushers in an age of peace.

=====The Double Death=====

After receiving the letter and hearing Shigetomo's final word, Naotaka—overcome with grief, guilt, and the weight of the forgiveness he had been offered—performed ''seppuku'' (ritual suicide) beside the dying Shogun. The act was performed with the assistance of [[Hotta Muneshige]], Naotaka's second (''kaishakunin''), who recorded the moment in what would become one of the most important primary sources of the era.

Hotta Muneshige, a philosopher of the ''kikungenpo'' tradition, recorded the moment of the double death in terms that would become central to Sanese cultural memory:

{{quote|The Shogun lay in the mud, his blood pooling beneath him. The Lord Naotaka knelt beside him, the bloodstained letter clutched to his chest. The Shogun spoke his final word—"Seal it"—and his breath ceased. The Lord Naotaka, with tears streaming down his face, drew his dagger and prepared to follow his lord in death. I, Muneshige, stood behind him, my sword raised. The Lord Naotaka spoke: "We were brothers once." And then he cut, and I cut, and it was done.}}

The double death—Shigetomo and Naotaka dying side by side in the mud of Ichijōdani—became the emotional climax of the Sengoku period. Two men who had been brothers in childhood, enemies in maturity, and reconciled in death, lay together in the mud, their blood mingling in the earth. The image would be depicted in countless paintings, dramatized in the great Taisen television dramas, and commemorated annually in the Red String Festival at Ichijōdani.

=====Deification=====

Following the double death, both men were deified by the Imperial Court:

* '''Honda Shigetomo''' was deified as the '''God of Seals''' (''Fūin no Kami''), the god of endings, closures, and the sealing of cycles. His deification reflected the significance of his final word—"Seal it"—as the act that ended the cycle of vengeance and ushered in the Pax Honda.
* '''Ii Naotaka''' was deified as the '''God of Vindication''' (''Benmei no Kami''), the god of justification and the clearing of names. His deification reflected the complexity of his role: he was both the assassin of an emperor and the recipient of that emperor's son's forgiveness, and his deification acknowledged the tragic complexity of his position.

The deification of both men—both the victim and the perpetrator—was a remarkable act of moral synthesis that embodied the spirit of the Great Reconciliation. It acknowledged that the cycle of violence had involved both guilt and forgiveness, both crime and mercy, and that the resolution of the cycle required the honoring of both sides.

===Honda Shigetoki and the Great Reconciliation (1625)===

{{Main|Honda Shigetoki|Great Reconciliation}}

The death of Shigetomo and Naotaka at Ichijōdani on June 18, 1625, left Okaiken in a precarious political position. The Shogun was dead, the leader of the Ii rebellion was dead, and the Emperor had been assassinated 12 days earlier. The Dual Sovereignty system, already strained by the Kinzō Incident, was on the verge of total collapse.

Into this vacuum stepped '''Honda Shigetoki''', the third son of Emperor Shirakawa and Shigetomo's younger brother. Shigetoki, who had been serving as a provincial governor in a distant part of the archipelago, rushed to Okami upon receiving news of the crisis. His arrival stabilized the political situation, and he was quickly acclaimed as the third Shogun.

Shigetoki's most significant act was the '''Great Reconciliation''' (1625), a comprehensive political settlement that resolved the crisis triggered by the Kinzō Incident and established the framework for the Pax Honda. The Great Reconciliation included:

* '''Amnesty for the Ii clan:''' The Ii clan, which had been in rebellion against the Shogunate, was granted a full amnesty. [[Ii Naoyoshi]], Naotaka's cousin who had accepted the amnesty terms, was permitted to rebuild the clan and retain a reduced domain. The amnesty was controversial—many Honda retainers saw it as a betrayal of Shigetomo's memory—but it was essential for ending the cycle of violence. The bloodstained letter, carried from Ichijōdani by Naoyoshi, was preserved as the most sacred treasure of the Ii clan, a physical embodiment of the forgiveness that the amnesty represented.
* '''Relocation of the Imperial Court to Okami:''' To prevent a repetition of the Kinzō Incident, the Imperial Court was relocated from Mitoyagi to Okami, eliminating the 500-kilometre gap between the Emperor and the Shogun that had enabled the assassination. The relocation was a fundamental restructuring of the Dual Sovereignty system, bringing the two sovereigns under the same roof and ensuring that future crises could be resolved through direct communication rather than delayed correspondence.
* '''Formal establishment of the Pax Honda:''' The Great Reconciliation established the framework for 260 years of peace. The Ii clan was reintegrated into the political order, the succession was stabilized, and the administrative reforms of the Kinzō era were continued in a modified form that was less threatening to the samurai class.
* '''Commission of the ''Mitsukoji'':''' Emperor Daigo (Prince Takahito, Emperor Shirakawa's eldest son and Shigetoki's older brother) commissioned the ''[[Mitsukoji]]''—the official chronicle of the Sengoku period and the Honda clan's rise to power. The ''Mitsukoji'', completed in 1680, would serve as the official history of the era for over three centuries, until the declassification of the Honda Letters in 2016 revealed its deliberate inaccuracies.

The Great Reconciliation was the moral resolution of 158 years of civil war. It was built on the foundation of Shigetomo's final act of mercy—the bloodstained letter and the command to "Seal it"—and it established the principle that the cycle of vengeance could be broken through forgiveness rather than destruction. The Pax Honda that followed was the longest period of peace in Sanese history, and its foundation in mercy rather than conquest remains one of the most remarkable achievements of the Honda clan.

====The Stone Pounding Cycle====

The Great Reconciliation and the events that produced it are often interpreted through the framework of the '''Stone Pounding Cycle''' (''Ishi-uchi no Wa''), a mythological concept from the mythology of Okaiken that describes the cyclical nature of history through three phases:

# '''Pounding''' (''Uchi''): The phase of warfare, conflict, and the hardening of institutional structures. The Sengoku period, with its 158 years of civil war, was the ultimate Pounding phase. The Honda clan's military campaigns—from Mazama to Tone—were the pounding of the stone, the violent process of forging a unified state from the fragmented archipelago.
# '''Loosening''' (''Yurumi''): The phase of collapse, when the hard institutional crust breaks down and the soft human reality beneath is exposed. The Kinzō Incident—the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa, the psychological deterioration of Shogun Shigetomo, the siege of Ichijōdani—was the Loosening, the moment when the ''tetsu-kage'' (the "iron shadow" of institutional design) cracked and the human frailty beneath was revealed.
# '''Release''' (''Kai''): The phase of unification and gratitude, when the cycle is completed and a new order is established through forgiveness rather than force. The Great Reconciliation, with its amnesty for the Ii clan and its foundation in Shigetomo's mercy, was the Release—the moment when the cycle of violence was sealed and a new era of peace began.

The Stone Pounding Cycle is also mapped onto the '''Niō Guardians'''—the two fierce protectors of Buddhist temples who stand at the gate:

* '''Agyō''' (the Open-Mouthed Guardian): The active, dynamic principle, associated with Pounding and with the Shogun. The Shogun, as the military and administrative authority, was the Agyō—the one who acts, who pounds, who enforces.
* '''Ungyō''' (the Closed-Mouthed Guardian): The still, receptive principle, associated with Release and with the Emperor. The Emperor, as the spiritual and ceremonial authority, was the Ungyō—the one who receives, who seals, who releases.

The Niō mapping provides a mythological framework for understanding the Dual Sovereignty system: the Shogun (Agyō) and the Emperor (Ungyō) are not rivals but complements, two halves of a single whole, whose combined action produces the complete cycle of Pounding, Loosening, and Release.

===The Honda Shogunate: Pax Honda (1625–1871)===

The Great Reconciliation established the framework for the '''Pax Honda'''—260 years of peace that lasted from 1625 to 1871. This period, named after the Honda clan's dominance, was the longest sustained peace in Sanese history and one of the longest in world history. During this period, Okaiken experienced significant cultural, economic, and administrative development, even as the political system remained fundamentally unchanged.

====The Shogunal Succession====

The Shogunal line faced several succession crises during the Pax Honda. The direct line of Shigetomo ended with the 9th Shogun, [[Honda Shigenari]], who died without a direct heir. The title then passed through a series of cadet branches:

* '''The Yamanaka Branch:''' Descended from Yamanaka Nobutaka, a younger brother of the 8th Shogun. The Yamanaka line produced the 9th Shogun (Shigenari), the 10th (Shigeharu), the 11th (Shigemura), the 12th (Shigetoshi), and the 13th (Shigesada), who died without an heir. The Yamanaka branch's tenure as the Shogunal line was marked by a gradual decline in the Shogun's personal authority and an increasing reliance on the bureaucratic apparatus of the Shogunate.
* '''The Fujisato Branch:''' Descended from the 3rd son of Emperor Shirakawa, Honda Shigetoki (the 3rd Shogun who had presided over the Great Reconciliation). The Fujisato line provided the final Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], who was adopted into the main line in the mid-19th century.

The succession crises of the Pax Honda were managed through the Shogunate's adoption system, which allowed the Shogunal line to continue even when direct heirs were lacking. This system, while effective at maintaining continuity, also contributed to the gradual weakening of the Shogun's personal authority, as successive Shoguns became increasingly dependent on the bureaucratic establishment and the cadet branches that had provided them.

====Administrative Development====

During the Pax Honda, the Shogunate's administrative system became increasingly sophisticated. The provincial governance system established by Shigetomo was refined and expanded, with provincial governors subject to regular review and transfer. The Okami Rice Exchange, established in 1624, became the economic center of the archipelago, setting prices and facilitating trade. The road network initiated under Shigetomo was expanded and maintained, providing the infrastructure for both commerce and governance.

The Pax Honda also saw the development of a distinctive Sanese urban culture, centered on the cities of Okami, Mitoyagi, and the castle towns of the provincial governors. This culture—characterized by its emphasis on aesthetics, literature, and the tea ceremony—was a product of the peace and prosperity that the Honda Shogunate provided, and it represented a significant departure from the martial culture of the Sengoku era.

====Sakoku (Closed Country Policy)====

During the Pax Honda, the Shogunate pursued a policy of ''sakoku''—the "closed country" policy that restricted foreign trade and contact. The policy was a continuation of the repression of Christianity initiated under Emperor Shirakawa, and it was designed to prevent the foreign influence that the Shogunate saw as a threat to its authority. The policy was not absolute—limited trade was permitted with certain partners—but it significantly limited Okaiken's engagement with the outside world for over two centuries.

===Modern Era (1870–Present)===

====The Saito Restoration (1870)====

The Pax Honda ended in 1870 with the [[Saito Restoration]], a political revolution that abolished the Shogunate and restored direct imperial rule. The last Shogun, '''Honda Yoshinobu''', voluntarily returned administrative power to his cousin, [[Saito of Mitoyagi|Emperor Saito]], in a peaceful transition that avoided the bloodshed that had accompanied the end of many historical regimes.

The Saito Restoration was a product of several factors: the increasing pressure of foreign powers, the growing obsolescence of the Shogunate's administrative system, and the rise of a reformist movement within the Honda clan itself. Yoshinobu, recognizing that the Shogunate had become an anachronism, chose to surrender power voluntarily rather than resist the inevitable. His decision was widely praised as an act of statesmanship that spared Okaiken the civil war that might otherwise have accompanied the transition.

Following the abolition of the Shogunate, the Honda family was incorporated into the ''Kazoku'' peerage system, with Yoshinobu receiving the title of Prince (''Kōshaku''). The Imperial line remained the heads of state, while the former Shogunal family transitioned into modern politics.

====20th Century: The Political Honda====

While the Imperial line remained the heads of state, the former Shogunal family transitioned seamlessly into modern politics. Several members of the Honda clan served as Prime Minister during the 20th and 21st centuries:

=====Honda Tadashige (Prime Minister, 1963–1966)=====

[[Honda Tadashige]], Yoshinobu's grandson, served as Prime Minister of Okaiken from 1963 to 1966. Tadashige oversaw the '''"Sanese Economic Miracle"'''—the period of rapid economic growth that transformed Okaiken from a war-devastated nation into a major industrial power. His economic policies, which emphasized export-led growth, infrastructure investment, and close cooperation between government and industry, established the framework for Okaiken's post-war economic success.

Tadashige's tenure as Prime Minister was marked by political stability and economic growth, but also by growing tensions within the coalition system that governed Okaiken. His resignation in 1966 was precipitated by a combination of political fatigue and the need to make way for a new generation of leaders.

=====Honda Shigehara (Prime Minister, 1990–1993)=====

[[Honda Shigehara]], Tadashige's brother, served as Prime Minister from 1990 to 1993. Shigehara's tenure was marked by the establishment of the current coalition system, in which the Progressive Party of Okaiken governs in coalition with smaller parties. His political reforms, which sought to modernize the electoral system and reduce the influence of factional politics, were controversial but ultimately successful in stabilizing the political landscape.

Shigehara's tenure also saw the beginning of the end of the Cold War and the reorientation of Okaiken's foreign policy toward greater engagement with the international community. His government negotiated several important trade agreements and established the framework for Okaiken's participation in international organizations.

====21st Century: Honda Shigetsuma (Prime Minister, 2021–Present)====

[[Honda Shigetsuma]], Tadashige's grandson and the current head of the Honda clan, has served as Prime Minister since 2021. Shigetsuma, a dominant figure in the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], continues the family's legacy of governance in the modern era. His tenure has been marked by efforts to address the economic and social challenges of the 21st century, including demographic decline, economic stagnation, and the need to adapt Okaiken's political institutions to the demands of a changing world.

Shigetsuma's leadership style is often compared to that of his ancestors: like Emperor Shirakawa, he combines strategic patience with a willingness to act decisively when necessary; like Shogun Shigetomo, he has shown a capacity for personal transformation and a willingness to choose mercy over force. His continued prominence in Sanese politics ensures that the Honda clan, nearly a millennium after its founding by the obscure retainer Honda Takakage, remains at the center of Okaiken's political life.

