==Cultural Legacy==

===The Honda Shitennō (Four Heavenly Pillars)===

The {{nihongo|'''Honda Shitennō'''|本田四天王|lit. "Four Heavenly Kings of Honda"}} were the four most distinguished military commanders serving under Honda Shigetada during the unification campaigns. Modeled on the classical concept of the Four Heavenly Kings of Buddhism, the Shitennō were the pillars of Honda military power and the instruments of Shigetada's conquest. The traditional roster includes:

* '''[[Ii Naomasa]]''' (c. 1561–1604): The "Red Devil" (''Aka-oni''), the preeminent cavalry commander of the era and Shigetada's closest military companion. Naomasa's Red Devil cavalry regiment was the most feared military unit in the archipelago, and his charge at the Battle of Tone was the decisive moment of the battle. He died on May 21, 1604, from an infected gunshot wound sustained at the Battle of Aonashiyama during the Ishijima Highland Campaign. His final words—"Do not be too generous with the wolves, my lord"—were widely interpreted as a warning about his own son, Naotaka, and as a prophecy of the Kinzō Incident.
* '''[[Anjō Motonobu]]''': A naval commander who distinguished himself at the Battle of Yamanote (1599) and in the Invasion of Ishijima. Motonobu's timely arrival with a squadron of warships turned the tide of the naval battle and ensured the success of the invasion.
* '''[[Sakakibara Yasumasa]]''': A siege warfare specialist who was killed leading an assault on the outer walls of Matsubara Castle during the Siege of Matsubara (1599–1600). His death was a significant loss to the Honda military machine, and his position in the Shitennō was filled by his successor.
* The '''fourth member''' of the Shitennō varies by source. Some accounts list [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] in this position, though his later assassination of Ogasawara Terumasa and his death as an enemy of the Honda make this attribution controversial. Other sources list a different commander whose identity is less well-established.

The Honda Shitennō represent the meritocratic ideal of Shigetatsu's reforms: each of the four was promoted based on ability rather than birth, and their collective achievement was the military foundation of Honda unification.

===The Bloodstained Letter===

The '''bloodstained letter''' (''chi no tegami'') is the single most sacred physical artifact in Sanese history. The letter, written by Shogun Honda Shigetomo in the final days before the Siege of Ichijōdani, is a formal decree offering Ii Naotaka the Higo domain—1 million ''koku'' in modern [[Kagami Prefecture]], in the deep south of the archipelago—and, more importantly, Shigetomo's personal forgiveness for the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa.

The letter exists in two versions:

* '''The first version''' (unsent): Found in Shigetomo's archives after his death, this version is aggressive and confrontational, demanding Naotaka's surrender and threatening total destruction. It represents the "Punisher" persona of Shigetomo's youth—the man who gave no quarter at the Battle of Nakasendo.
* '''The second version''' (sent): The version Shigetomo actually carried with him to Ichijōdani, this is a formal decree offering the Higo domain and a personal letter of forgiveness. It represents the merciful Shogun of Shigetomo's maturity—the man who had learned the mercy lesson and carried it to its ultimate conclusion.

The second version was soaked in Shigetomo's blood when he was shot at the castle gate on June 18, 1625. The blood seeped through the leather pouch in which the letter was carried and saturated the paper, making portions of the text illegible. The letter was retrieved by Naotaka as Shigetomo lay dying, and was preserved by [[Ii Naoyoshi]] as the most sacred treasure of the Ii clan after the Great Reconciliation.

The physical artifact—a blood-soaked, mud-stained document—was kept in the Ii clan's private archives for nearly four centuries. It was not until the declassification of the Honda Letters on April 8, 2016, that the letter was made available to scholars. A research team spent two years deciphering the blood-obscured text, using infrared imaging and other modern techniques to recover the original writing beneath the bloodstains. The deciphered text confirmed the account given in the ''Honda Letters'' and provided the first physical evidence of the mercy that Shigetomo had offered Naotaka at the moment of his death.

===The Honda Letters (Declassified 2016)===

{{Main|Honda Letters}}

The '''Honda Letters''' are the personal correspondence of Shogun Honda Shigetomo, written between May 18 and June 17, 1625, and declassified on April 8, 2016. The letters are the most significant primary source for the final weeks of the Sengoku period, and their declassification revolutionized scholarly understanding of the Kinzō Incident, the Siege of Ichijōdani, and Shigetomo's psychological state during the final crisis of his life.

The letters were preserved by the Honda clan as private family documents and were not available to scholars until 2016. Their declassification revealed that the official ''Mitsukoji'' chronicle (1680) contained deliberate inaccuracies designed to protect the reputations of several key figures. The Honda Letters correct the record in several important respects:

* They reveal the full extent of Shigetomo's psychological deterioration following the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa, which the ''Mitsukoji'' had minimized.
* They provide the text of both versions of the bloodstained letter—the aggressive first version and the merciful second version—confirming that Shigetomo's final act was a conscious choice of mercy over force.
* They document the three stages of Shigetomo's psychological state: Stage 1 (May 18–June 5), bureaucratic frenzy; Stage 2 (June 6–15), psychotic breakdown; and Stage 3 (June 16–17), the final 48 hours of eerie clarity and resolution.
* They reveal Shigetomo's development of the ''tetsu-kage'' concept—the "iron shadow" of institutional design that conceals the soft silt of human frailty—as a framework for understanding the gap between political systems and the human beings who operate them.

The Honda Letters are now recognized as the most important primary source for the final phase of the Sengoku period, and their declassification in 2016 marked a fundamental shift in the scholarly understanding of the era's climactic events.

===The Mitsukoji (1680)===

{{Main|Mitsukoji}}

The '''''Mitsukoji''''' (1680) is the official chronicle of the Sengoku period and the Honda clan's rise to power, commissioned by Emperor Daigo (Prince Takahito, Emperor Shirakawa's eldest son) as part of the Great Reconciliation's program of historical documentation. The chronicle was completed in 1680, 55 years after the events it describes, and it served as the official history of the era for over three centuries.

The ''Mitsukoji'' is a comprehensive work that covers the entire Sengoku period, from the mid-15th century to the Great Reconciliation of 1625. Its structure follows a thematic framework that organizes the historical narrative around the key concepts of the era: the Stone Pounding Cycle, the Dual Sovereignty system, the mercy lesson, and the Pax Honda. The chronicle's methodology emphasizes the moral dimensions of historical events, interpreting the Sengoku period as a cycle of violence that was ultimately resolved through mercy and forgiveness.

However, the ''Mitsukoji'' contains deliberate inaccuracies that were designed to protect the reputations of several key figures. These inaccuracies, which were not recognized until the declassification of the Honda Letters in 2016, include:

* The minimization of Shigetomo's psychological deterioration during the Kinzō Incident, which was portrayed as a period of calm resolve rather than the psychotic breakdown documented in the Honda Letters.
* The omission of the first (aggressive) version of the bloodstained letter, which was excluded to preserve the image of Shigetomo as a consistently merciful figure.
* The attribution of the shot that killed Shigetomo to "an unknown soldier," rather than acknowledging the possibility that Naotaka himself may have been the shooter.
* The simplification of the Ii clan's motivations, which were portrayed as simple ambition rather than the complex mix of political grievance, personal loyalty, and tragic circumstance documented in the Honda Letters.

The declassification of the Honda Letters in 2016 prompted a comprehensive reassessment of the ''Mitsukoji'' and its role in shaping the historical record. Modern scholarship now treats the ''Mitsukoji'' as a valuable but biased source, whose inaccuracies must be corrected through comparison with the Honda Letters and other primary sources.

===The Red String Festival===

The '''Red String Festival''' (''Akai Ito Matsuri'') is an annual festival held at [[Ichijōdani]] to commemorate the double death of Shogun Honda Shigetomo and Ii Naotaka on June 18, 1625. The festival is one of the most culturally significant events in Okaiken, attracting thousands of participants and spectators each year.

The festival's central ritual involves a thick hemp rope, dyed red, that is strung between the two graves of Shigetomo and Naotaka, which face each other across a narrow path. The graves were constructed after the Great Reconciliation to honor both men—the Shogun and the man who killed him—and their facing position symbolizes the complex relationship between them: brothers, enemies, and ultimately, reconciled in death.

During the festival, a participant dressed as a "demon" (''oni'') approaches the red string and cuts it with a ceremonial sword. The cutting of the string symbolizes the severing of the cycle of vengeance—the "sealing" that Shigetomo commanded with his final word, ''fūjiro''. After the string is cut, the temple gate between the two graves is opened, symbolizing the passage from the cycle of violence to the era of peace.

The Red String Festival is a powerful cultural expression of the moral themes that define the Honda clan's legacy: the cycle of violence and its resolution through mercy, the bond between brothers that endures beyond betrayal, and the sealing of the past that makes the future possible. The festival has been held annually for nearly four centuries and is recognized as one of Okaiken's most important cultural traditions.

===Taisen Dramas===

The Honda clan's history has been the subject of numerous ''Taisen'' dramas—the annual historical television series produced by the Sanese broadcasting network. These dramas, which dramatize the lives of historical figures for a mass audience, have played a significant role in shaping popular understanding of the Honda clan and the Sengoku period.

Two Taisen dramas are particularly notable:

* '''''Honda Shigetomo''''' (1983): This dramatization of Shogun Shigetomo's life arc—from the "Punisher" of his youth to the tragic martyr of Ichijōdani—achieved a '''90.4% audience rating''' for its final episode, one of the highest ratings in Sanese television history. The drama's final scene, depicting the double death at Ichijōdani, is widely regarded as one of the most powerful moments in Sanese television, and its emotional impact helped cement Shigetomo's place in the popular imagination as a figure of tragic heroism and moral grandeur.
* '''''Honda Tenno''''' (2014): This dramatization of Emperor Shirakawa's life—from the stolen prince of the Yuzawa Incident to the unifier of Okaiken—was notable for its comprehensive treatment of Shigetada's military campaigns and its nuanced portrayal of the Dual Sovereignty system. The drama was praised for its historical accuracy and its willingness to engage with the complex political and moral questions raised by Shigetada's reign.

These and other Taisen dramas have played a crucial role in maintaining the Honda clan's cultural prominence. By dramatizing the clan's history for mass audiences, they have ensured that the moral and political themes of the Sengoku era—the cycle of violence, the mercy lesson, the Dual Sovereignty system, and the Pax Honda—remain part of the living cultural memory of Okaiken.

===The Ii Clan Connection: From Rivalry to Ikyo===

The Honda clan's relationship with the [[Ii clan]] is one of the most complex and consequential in Sanese history. The two clans were bound together by the brotherhood of their founders—Shigetada and Naomasa, who met in a Nagashino weapons shop in 1589—and by the parallel brotherhood of their sons—Shigetomo and Naotaka, who met as child hostages at Otomo Castle in 1590. These bonds, forged in friendship, were tested by political rivalry and ultimately broken by the Kinzō Incident, only to be restored through the Great Reconciliation and the mercy of Ichijōdani.

The Ii clan's modern legacy is a remarkable story of recovery and transformation. After the Great Reconciliation, [[Ii Naoyoshi]], who had accepted the amnesty terms, rebuilt the clan from its reduced domain. Over the following centuries, the Ii clan gradually rebuilt its economic and political position, and in the modern era, the clan's commercial activities culminated in the establishment of the '''Ii Holdings Company''' in 1955.

Ii Holdings merged with the Onkyo corporation to form '''[[Ikyo]]''', now one of the world's leading technology conglomerates. The transformation of the Ii clan from a defeated rebel house to the family behind a global technology empire is one of the most remarkable commercial trajectories in modern history, and it stands as a testament to the enduring legacy of the Great Reconciliation, which allowed the Ii clan to rebuild and prosper rather than being destroyed.

The relationship between the Honda and Ii clans in the modern era is one of mutual respect and shared history. The bloodstained letter, preserved by the Ii clan as its most sacred treasure, is a physical embodiment of the bond between the two houses—a bond that was forged in childhood friendship, tested by war and betrayal, and ultimately sealed in the mud of Ichijōdani.

